<h1 class=3D"box">Polynomial Expressions for Delta T (&#916;T)</h1>

<h2 class=3D"box">Adapted from "Five Millennium Canon of Solar Eclipses" [E=
spenak and Meeus]</h2>


<p class=3D"quote">
Using the &#916;T values derived from the historical record and from direct=
 observations (see: <a href=3D"https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEhelp/deltat2=
004.html#tab1"> Table 1 </a> and <a href=3D"https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/S=
Ehelp/deltat2004.html#tab2"> Table 2 </a>), a series of polynomial expressi=
ons have been created to simplify the evaluation of &#916;T for any time du=
ring the interval -1999 to +3000. </p>
<p class=3D"quote">
We define the decimal year "y" as follows:</p>
<pre>		y =3D year + (month - 0.5)/12
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
This gives "y" for the middle of the month, which is accurate enough given =
the precision in the known values of &#916;T.=20
The following polynomial expressions can be used calculate the value of &#9=
16;T (in seconds) over the time period covered by of the <a href=3D"https:/=
/eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEpubs/5MCSE.html">Five Millennium Canon of Solar Ec=
lipses: -1999 to +3000</a>.</p>
<p class=3D"quote">
Before the year -500, calculate:</p>
<pre>		&#916;T =3D -20 + 32 * u^2
		where:	u =3D (year-1820)/100
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
Between years -500 and +500, we use the data from <a href=3D"https://eclips=
e.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEhelp/deltat2004.html#tab1">Table 1</a>, except that for t=
he year -500 we changed the value 17190 to 17203.7 in order to avoid a disc=
ontinuity with the previous formula at that epoch.=20
The value for &#916;T is given by a polynomial of the 6th degree, which rep=
roduces the values in <a href=3D"https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEhelp/delta=
t2004.html#tab1">Table 1</a> with an error not larger than 4 seconds:</p>
<pre>	&#916;T =3D 10583.6 - 1014.41 * u + 33.78311 * u^2 - 5.952053 * u^3
		- 0.1798452 * u^4 + 0.022174192 * u^5 + 0.0090316521 * u^6=20
	where: u =3D y/100
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
Between years +500 and +1600, we again use the data from <a href=3D"https:/=
/eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEhelp/SEhelp/deltat2004.html#tab1">Table 1</a> to d=
erive a polynomial of the 6th degree.</p>
<pre>	&#916;T =3D 1574.2 - 556.01 * u + 71.23472 * u^2 + 0.319781 * u^3
		- 0.8503463 * u^4 - 0.005050998 * u^5 + 0.0083572073 * u^6
	where: u =3D (y-1000)/100
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
Between years +1600 and +1700, calculate:</p>
<pre>	&#916;T =3D 120 - 0.9808 * t - 0.01532 * t^2 + t^3 / 7129
	where:  t =3D y - 1600
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
Between years +1700 and +1800, calculate:</p>
<pre>	&#916;T =3D 8.83 + 0.1603 * t - 0.0059285 * t^2 + 0.00013336 * t^3 - =
t^4 / 1174000
	where: t =3D y - 1700
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
Between years +1800 and +1860, calculate:</p>
<pre>	&#916;T =3D 13.72 - 0.332447 * t + 0.0068612 * t^2 + 0.0041116 * t^3 =
- 0.00037436 * t^4=20
		+ 0.0000121272 * t^5 - 0.0000001699 * t^6 + 0.000000000875 * t^7
	where: t =3D y - 1800
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
Between years 1860 and 1900, calculate:</p>
<pre>	&#916;T =3D 7.62 + 0.5737 * t - 0.251754 * t^2 + 0.01680668 * t^3
		-0.0004473624 * t^4 + t^5 / 233174
	where: t =3D y - 1860
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
Between years 1900 and 1920, calculate:</p>
<pre>	&#916;T =3D -2.79 + 1.494119 * t - 0.0598939 * t^2 + 0.0061966 * t^3 =
- 0.000197 * t^4
	where: t =3D y - 1900
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
Between years 1920 and 1941, calculate:</p>
<pre>	&#916;T =3D 21.20 + 0.84493*t - 0.076100 * t^2 + 0.0020936 * t^3
	where: t =3D y - 1920
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
Between years 1941 and 1961, calculate:</p>
<pre>	&#916;T =3D 29.07 + 0.407*t - t^2/233 + t^3 / 2547
	where: t =3D y - 1950
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
Between years 1961 and 1986, calculate:</p>
<pre>	&#916;T =3D 45.45 + 1.067*t - t^2/260 - t^3 / 718
	where: t =3D y - 1975
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
Between years 1986 and 2005, calculate:</p>
<pre>	&#916;T =3D 63.86 + 0.3345 * t - 0.060374 * t^2 + 0.0017275 * t^3 + 0=
.000651814 * t^4=20
		+ 0.00002373599 * t^5
	where: t =3D y - 2000
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
Between years 2005 and 2050, calculate:</p>
<pre>	&#916;T =3D 62.92 + 0.32217 * t + 0.005589 * t^2
	where: t =3D y - 2000
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
This expression is derived from estimated values of &#916;T in the years 20=
10 and 2050.=20
The value for 2010 (66.9 seconds) is based on a linearly extrapolation from=
 2005 using 0.39 seconds/year (average from 1995 to 2005).=20
The value for 2050 (93 seconds) is linearly extrapolated from 2010 using 0.=
66 seconds/year (average rate from 1901 to 2000).</p>
<p class=3D"quote">
Between years 2050 and 2150, calculate:</p>
<pre>	&#916;T =3D -20 + 32 * ((y-1820)/100)^2 - 0.5628 * (2150 - y)
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
The last term is introduced to eliminate the discontinuity at 2050.</p>
<p class=3D"quote">
After 2150, calculate:</p>
<pre>	&#916;T =3D -20 + 32 * u^2
	where:	u =3D (year-1820)/100
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
All values of &#916;T based on Morrison and Stephenson [2004] assume a valu=
e for the Moon's secular acceleration of -26 arcsec/cy^2.=20
However, the ELP-2000/82 lunar ephemeris employed in the <a href=3D"https:/=
/eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEpubs/5MCSE.html">Canon</a> uses a slightly differe=
nt value of -25.858 arcsec/cy^2.=20
Thus, a small correction "c" must be added to the values derived from the p=
olynomial expressions for &#916;T before they can be used in the <a href=3D=
"https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEpubs/5MCSE.html">Canon</a></p>
<pre>	c =3D -0.000012932 * (y - 1955)^2
</pre>
<p class=3D"quote">
Since the values of &#916;T for the interval 1955 to 2005 were derived inde=
pendent of any lunar ephemeris, no correction is needed for this period.</p=
>

<p class=3D"quote">
The <a href=3D"https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEhelp/uncertainty2004.html">u=
ncertainty in &#916;T</a> over this period can be estimated from scatter in=
 the measurements.
</p>

